-# The DevTools Times
+# smallpt — browser edition
 
-This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
+Kevin Beason’s open-source [smallpt](https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/) C++ path tracer, compiled to WebAssembly with Emscripten 7.0.0. The homepage is prerendered to `dist/client/index.html`.
 
+## Build
+
+The compiled `.wasm` and ES-module loader are included, so a normal application build does not require Emscripten:
 
 ```sh
-pnpm install && pnpm run start
+pnpm install
+npm run build
 ```
 
-The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
+To rebuild C++ after changing `native/smallpt.cpp`, [install and activate Emscripten](https://emscripten.org/docs/getting_started/downloads.html), then run:
+
+```sh
+npm run build:all
+# Or: EMXX=/path/to/em++ npm run build:all
+```
+
+`npm run build:wasm` rebuilds only the Wasm assets. No server-side rendering is involved in tracing. The existing Astro application retains its Node adapter for other routes; the playground itself and everything in `dist/client` can be statically hosted. Serve `.wasm` files as `application/wasm` for streaming compilation; Emscripten also falls back to ArrayBuffer compilation. Browser ES modules and Workers require HTTP(S).
+
+## Rendering
+
+`public/wasm/render-worker.js` loads the actual C++ module in a dedicated module Worker. The C++ renderer accumulates four tent-filtered subpixel samples per pass and exposes an RGBA buffer; the worker transfers snapshots to the canvas. No SharedArrayBuffer or special isolation headers are needed. A 1 MB Wasm stack accommodates recursive ray tracing.
+
+The original ray/sphere intersections, diffuse/mirror/glass materials, camera, and Monte Carlo radiance algorithm are retained. The adaptation replaces the original CLI/PPM output and OpenMP loop with an incremental C ABI, uses progressive accumulation with final gamma correction, and adds seeded random tinting for the two foreground spheres and the side walls. Reset restores the original scene. Changing settings or randomizing terminates the previous worker.
+
+The initial PNG is real output generated by the compiled Wasm module (640 × 480, 256 samples/pixel), not a stock image or mock rendering.
+
+## Checks and preview generation
+
+```sh
+npm run check:wasm
+node scripts/render-preview.mjs
+```
 
-## Tech Stack
+The first command validates the binary, renders pixels directly with Wasm, checks RGBA output and repeatable seeds, checks randomization and reset, and exercises the actual worker module’s loading and message flow. The second also regenerates the initial PNG using `sharp`. These scripts use Node only and start no server. The worker-specific loader is instantiated with a local binary through Emscripten’s `instantiateWasm` hook.
 
-- **[Astro](https://astro.build/)**: The web framework for building content-driven websites.
-- **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
-- **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
-- **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+Original source and MIT license are preserved in `native/smallpt.original.cpp` and `native/LICENSE.txt`.
